R-37 — name the MOST SEVERE hazard, not the first in registration order

WHY THIS IS A PATCH FILE AND NOT A COMMIT
-----------------------------------------
It edits orca/policy.py, which CLAUDE.md rule 5 freezes ("Do not modify
orca/schema.py or orca/policy.py once tests pass, unless explicitly
asked") and which PRD N-5 restates. PRD §5 also records a deliberate
decision to leave R-37 open there: orca/planner.py's _zone_verdict()
already uses max() on the path written for R-59, with the note "R-37
stays open in the frozen policy.py by decision".

So this is not something to apply on a generic "fix the bugs" — it needs
a human to decide. Everything needed to make that decision is below.
Applying it is one command; so is walking away from it.

WHAT IS WRONG
-------------
policy.py picks the finding it names by LIST ORDER:

    primary        = hard_denials[0]
    primary_danger = danger[0]

AGENTS is [eo_satellite, ocean_state, weather, hazard, geofence], so
weather_agent is ALWAYS considered before hazard_agent. When both cross
the 0.6 threshold, ORCA reports the wind and never mentions the sea.

MEASURED, on the real data/cache/ of 2026-08-28, forcing 2.4 m waves
(deliberately just BELOW the 2.5 m Douglas 4/5 hard deny, so the crew is
one step from the limit and most needs to be told):

    zone              names            actual worst              verdict
    Point Calimere    wind  0.70       hazard_agent  0.96        WRONG
    Mandapam          wind  0.74       hazard_agent  0.96        WRONG
    Rameswaram        wind  0.95       hazard_agent  0.96        WRONG
    Thoothukudi       wind  0.66       hazard_agent  0.96        WRONG

    4 of the 6 zones carrying more than one danger named the wrong
    hazard. After this patch: 6 of 6 name the worst one.

The same list-order pick is in rule 1: with a wave hard-deny AND a
geofence hard-deny, hazard_agent (index 3) is named and the fact that the
boat is inside a marine national park is never said.

WHY max() IS SAFE HERE
----------------------
  * It changes WHICH finding is quoted. It never changes the ACTION.
    The set `danger` and the set `hard_denials` are unchanged, so every
    GO / DO NOT GO / SAFER ALTERNATIVE / CANNOT ASSESS verdict is
    byte-identical before and after.
  * Python's max() returns the FIRST maximum, so an exact tie keeps
    today's registration order. This is a strict refinement, never a
    reshuffle of equally severe findings.
  * zone_summaries already does exactly this (PRD R-18, "a single hazard
    must not be diluted"), and _zone_verdict()'s R-59 path already does
    it too. This makes the third site agree with the other two rather
    than introducing a new rule.
  * policy.py stays deterministic, LLM-free and network-free
    (CLAUDE.md rule 4). No new imports, no new dependencies (rule 6).

